WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.150
So in the last decade, there's
been a sudden explosion

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.410
in teenage girls
identifying as transgender.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.820
So my book explores why.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.290
Abigail Shrier's "Irreversible Damage:

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:14.697
The Transgender Craze
Seducing Our Daughters"

00:00:14.697 --> 00:00:18.810
was one of last year's most
celebrated and condemned books.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.090
It showed up in year-end
lists of top books,

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.170
but was also banned by Target.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.670
And her publisher was disallowed
from buying ads on Amazon.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.260
Abigail Shrier's book
is a dangerous polemic

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.640
with a goal of making people not trans,

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.960
wrote an American Civil Liberties
Union attorney on Twitter.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.990
We have to fight these
ideas which are leading

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.690
to the criminalization
of trans life again.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.950
A lawyer by training, Shrier
says she fully supports

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.200
the rights of adults to undergo
gender reassignment surgery.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.020
She's concerned that teenage girls

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:50.572
are making irrevocable
changes to their bodies

00:00:50.572 --> 00:00:54.020
that in coming years they
might wish they could reverse.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.370
In a new paperback edition
of "Irreversible Damage"

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.860
Shrier follows up on several
of the women she spoke with

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.140
and details her experiences
of being deplatformed.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.184
Reason spoke with Shrier
about the controversy

00:01:06.184 --> 00:01:08.971
over her book, the Streisand effect,

00:01:08.971 --> 00:01:11.230
and the future of free expression

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:14.300
in an increasingly polarized
cultural landscape.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.290
Abigail Shrier, thanks
for talking to Reason.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.123
Thank you.

00:01:17.123 --> 00:01:20.150
Give me the elevator pitch
for "Irreversible Damage:

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:23.987
The Transgender Craze
Seducing Our Daughters."

00:01:23.987 --> 00:01:28.490
So in the last decade, there's
been a sudden explosion

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:32.371
really in teenage girls
identifying as transgender.

00:01:32.371 --> 00:01:34.790
We've never seen anything
like this before.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:38.120
The underlying condition, gender dysphoria

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.659
or severe discomfort
in one's biological sex

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:43.380
is something we've known
about for 100 years.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.150
And it always predominantly
afflicted boys and men.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.530
And now in the last
decade, it's overwhelmingly

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:52.080
teenage girls with no childhood
history of gender dysphoria.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.760
So my book explores, why, what's going on?

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.480
Why do so many young teenage girls

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.280
suddenly decide that they
want to leave womanhood?

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.710
And why are so many doctors

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:04.845
and therapists and teachers helping them?

00:02:04.845 --> 00:02:07.951
So let's clarify a couple things.

00:02:07.951 --> 00:02:11.380
You say historically, the kind of move

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:13.520
to have gender dysphoria or the idea

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.210
that you're born in the wrong body

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.291
or you're uncomfortable
in the body you're in,

00:02:17.291 --> 00:02:21.034
typically that has been mostly
men wanting to be women.

00:02:21.034 --> 00:02:22.380
Yes.
Yeah.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.190
Overwhelmingly, I mean the

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:25.340
Do we know why that is?

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.727
Or what do people say, why is that?

00:02:27.727 --> 00:02:29.060
People have asked me that.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:30.580
You know, I'm not sure that we do.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.900
There are some attempts to do brain scans

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:37.900
and find neurological
differences, but, you know,

00:02:38.453 --> 00:02:42.765
for whatever reason, this did
overwhelmingly afflict boys

00:02:42.765 --> 00:02:47.540
and men and so I think there's, you know,

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:52.393
they estimate 0.01% of men
are naturally transgender.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:58.547
That's what the DSM5
estimated, and for women it's

00:02:58.547 --> 00:03:00.870
Which is the manual that
talks about, I mean,

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.390
it's the psychological,
psychiatric document of things.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:05.223
Oh yeah, that's right.

00:03:05.223 --> 00:03:09.520
The psychiatric manual,
the DSM5 and for women,

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.727
it was 0.003, so you're
talking about tiny,

00:03:12.727 --> 00:03:16.460
tiny percentages, one in 30,000 women.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:17.920
So basically no one you would have gone

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.060
to high school with in the last decade.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:23.070
Now we have more than 2%
of high school students.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:26.590
Their recent, most recent
surveys are even higher

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:27.863
identifying as transgender.

00:03:27.863 --> 00:03:31.002
And we know they're
overwhelmingly teenage girls.

00:03:31.002 --> 00:03:33.020
So whenever you see, yeah.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.200
Yeah, I'm sorry, I
didn't mean to interrupt.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.430
No, whenever you see something like that,

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:37.810
it's just worth asking why.

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:39.770
And that's what my book does.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:44.517
So when you say these are the
overwhelmingly teenage girls

00:03:44.517 --> 00:03:47.120
and that it is, you know,

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.379
they haven't presented this before.

00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:51.983
I have a good friend,
a contributing editor

00:03:51.983 --> 00:03:56.442
at "Reason" magazine, Deirdre
McCloskey, who is transgender

00:03:56.442 --> 00:04:00.700
and who transitioned in her
50s or around the age of 50.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.930
And she talked about
knowing from the beginning

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.980
that she was a woman, even
though she was in a man's body.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.460
I mean, she played football
in college, et cetera.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.470
But what you're talking about are girls

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:14.570
who generally don't have,
or the specific subset

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:18.140
you're talking about are
girls who don't have a history

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:21.730
of always saying, I want to be male.

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:24.500
Yes, that's one of the
peculiar sort of features of it

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.770
is that this always
began in early childhood

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:28.830
for those who had it.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.140
It's a real condition.
Right.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.220
And now it's teenage girls
with no childhood history,

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.510
meaning they were perfectly comfortable

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.570
playing in princess
dresses, never, you know,

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:41.320
very often these girls liked ballet

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:43.500
and all those sort of girly things.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.416
And then they hit their teenage years.

00:04:45.416 --> 00:04:47.872
And this is the other peculiar feature,

00:04:47.872 --> 00:04:50.875
with their girlfriends
because it very often happens

00:04:50.875 --> 00:04:53.940
in peer groups, decide that
they're really transgender

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.518
and they start clamoring
for hormones and surgeries.

00:04:56.518 --> 00:04:59.750
And unfortunately today they're very,

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.750
very easily obtaining them with
almost no medical oversight.

